# THE EXPENSIVE CHECKS -- pipe-separated, one row per check. Columns:
#
#   check | command | minutes | due_after | artefact |
#   last_run | last_commit | last_result | note | on_surprise
#
# ``tools/cadence.py`` reads this file. ``./check.ps1`` prints what is due at
# the end of every run, and the handoff skill will not close a session that
# leaves a due check unnamed in NEXT.md.
#
# ## THE COLUMNS
#
# check       the name passed to ``--record``.
# command     exactly what to run. Copy it; do not paraphrase it.
# minutes     measured, not estimated. A number here comes from a run.
# due_after   commits on main since the last run before this is owed. The clock
#             is CHANGES, not days: a quiet fortnight owes nothing and five
#             sessions in a day owe the suite.
# artefact    the committed file this check writes, if it writes one. With it
#             filled in the last run is DERIVED -- ``git log -1 -- <artefact>``
#             -- and the three stamp columns are ignored and stay empty. That is
#             the whole reason the sweep needs no bookkeeping and the suite does.
# last_run    ISO date of the last ``--record``, for a row with no artefact.
# last_commit the short hash it was recorded at. ``never`` is DUE, not clean.
# last_result pass | fail. A FAILING check is recorded as failing. Stamping it
#             green to get a clean board is the one thing this file cannot
#             survive.
# note        what a failure was, in enough detail for the next session to
#             start from it rather than re-running to find out.
# on_surprise where the fix goes when the result is not what was expected. The
#             moment a 30-minute suite comes back red is the worst moment to be
#             working that out from scratch, so it is worked out in advance.
#
# ## WHY A LEDGER AND NOT A SCHEDULE
#
# A schedule would run these; this only says what is owed. The full suite is
# thirty minutes on the user's own machine and the standing rule is to ask
# first. What the rule was missing is a way to know how long it has been -- so
# a session can do a lot of work cheaply, and still hand over an honest account
# of what it did not run.
#
suite | python -m pytest -q | 30 | 4 |  | 2026-09-12 | e82f209 | pass | 1301 passed in 28m51s, first ever recorded run. An earlier run of the same session failed 3 (test_named_routes, test_vanillin, test_detailed_balance); all three pinned the forward-only discovery defect T7 removed and were restated, not silenced. | the failing test names its own module; a green ./check.ps1 bounds 56 smoke tests and nothing else, so start from this session's diff, not from the test
tolerance | python validation/tolerance_audit.py | 10 | 6 |  | 2026-09-12 | e82f209 | fail | first ever run, 11m12s. 2 quotable digits move with tolerance (activity 0.128%, multistep_prep 0.107%) and named_routes raises at rtol 1e-8 -- all three PRE-EXISTING: both examples print byte-identical output on pre-T7 and post-T7 source, and the raise is diagnosed in-run as older than S13. Fix is per-example tight tolerance, as lime_cycle.py does. | a moved trajectory is numerics/vessel_integrator.py or the tolerances in numerics/integrator.py; a moved EQUILIBRIUM is thermochemistry, never the solver -- see rule 9
reachable | python tools/build_reachable.py | 35 | 10 | data/catalog/derived/reachable.psv |  |  |  |  | a changed reaction total is whichever of the four busiest templates moved; a changed templates_fired is the real finding and belongs in silent_templates.psv
routes | python examples/named_routes.py | 1 | 4 |  | 2026-09-13 | 97f33b0 | pass | T18: 17 routes ran in 34.5 s, exit 0, and the output is byte-identical to the same example on pre-T18 source once clock stamps are stripped -- no deep chain holds a plateau carboxylate. Also green: test_playable, test_granularity, test_protonation, test_solids_and_ions, test_detailed_balance, test_named_routes, test_phosphate, test_element_solids, test_matter, test_formation_data, test_standard_state, test_precipitation, test_shelf, test_reachable. | a route that stops ending is its own step's template or a species that lost its price; run it with the route named before touching anything
playable | python tools/build_playable.py | 1 | 8 | data/catalog/PLAYABLE.md |  |  |  |  | regenerate, never edit; a moved tier count is a shelf row or a route_steps class, and check.ps1 -Full catches staleness already
